\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Arpitan (Franco-Proven\c{c}al) is spoken in eastern France, Romandy and north-western Italy. It is classed as severely endangered by UNESCO. In France it has never been an optional school language. Search results were thin and mostly general.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item Acad\'emie de Grenoble regional-languages discovery week document, with a La Fontaine fable in Dauphin\'e Franco-Proven\c{c}al and a song with texts: \url{https://savoie-educ.web.ac-grenoble.fr/sites/default/files/Media/document/semaine_langues_langues-regionales.pdf}
\item BnF catalogue record for an introductory method with a small Franco-Proven\c{c}al--French dictionary, using the graphy of an international working group: \url{https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb475034871}
\end{itemize}
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Linguistic overview of Franco-Proven\c{c}al (HAL): \url{https://halshs.archives-ouvertes.fr/ensl-00632464v1/file/LC18_francoprovencal.pdf}
\item Simple English Wikipedia, Arpitan language: \url{https://simple.wikipedia.com/wiki/Arpitan_language}
\end{itemize}
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None specifically identified in search results.
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
None specifically identified.
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
There is no established school curriculum. Teaching depends on local initiatives; the Grenoble materials are a starting point. Dialect and spelling variation is large.
\end{document}
